\chapter{Structural macros and local syntax}
\label{ch:39}
\index{Structural macros and local syntax}

Structural macro patterns can match nested syntax and collect repetitions. Capture rank records how a bound value sits within repetition dimensions. A template repetition must be driven by compatible capture widths. Empty repetitions must carry their shape so an empty inner list does not look like a missing binding. Matching and copying are budgeted separately.

Local \OnxCodeTerm{let\_syntax} binds transformers against the outer syntax environment. Recursive \OnxCodeTerm{letrec\_syntax} can refer through the group. The implementation tracks definition and caller contexts, then lowers to collision-checked identifiers and erases the local transformer forms before wire transport. For a regression fixture, use one empty inner repetition and another with two elements. Add an unrelated caller binder with the same name as an introduced temporary. These cases distinguish capture rank, width handling, and hygiene from simple token substitution.

\section{Keep the empty row on your scratchpad}
For the shipped invocation \OnxCodeTerm{rows!([1, 2], [], [3])}, give the expression capture two coordinates: row number and position within that row. The outer match has width three. The corresponding inner widths are two, zero, and one. Walk the template one row at a time. The first iteration emits a print for 1 and a print for 2. The next emits no print. The last emits one for 3. Flattening all captured leaves at match time could reproduce this particular output by accident, but it would lose the evidence needed to drive a template that did something once per row. In particular a missing second row and a present second row of width zero would become indistinguishable.

Scope also has structure that a flat list of tokens cannot record. In the local syntax example, the definition of \OnxCodeTerm{answer} mentions the outer value of \OnxCodeTerm{base}, namely 40. Inside the use block a new binding sets the same spelling to zero. The call with 2 still prints 42: the free name in the transformer body was written at its definition. Now trace \OnxCodeTerm{outer\_helper!()} in the nested block. Its reference to the outer transformer produces 1, while the adjacent direct call of the newly bound helper produces 99. These two calls test the syntax environment itself, independently of the earlier example's value binding.

For the recursive local group, follow the call from first to second: second adds two to 40. That forward reference is precisely why the two definitions appear in the same recursive syntax group.

